# uname — real-world examples

## Print system information

```sh
# Print all system information
uname -a
# Linux homelab 6.9.7-1-default #1 SMP PREEMPT_DYNAMIC openSUSE Tumbleweed x86_64 GNU/Linux

# Print the hostname
uname -n
# homelab

# Print the kernel release
uname -r
# 6.9.7-1-default

# Print the kernel version, with more specific information
uname -v

# Print the hardware architecture
uname -m
# x86_64

# Print the kernel name
uname -s
# Linux

# Print the operating system
uname -o
# GNU/Linux
```

## Homelab uses

```sh
# Did the last zypper dup install a new kernel? Compare running vs installed
uname -r
rpm -q --last kernel-default | head -1

# Check the architecture before grabbing a binary release
uname -m    # x86_64 -> download the amd64 build

# Quick kernel survey across the lab
for h in homelab mediaserver; do echo -n "$h: "; ssh david@$h uname -r; done

# Use the kernel release in a script path
ls /lib/modules/$(uname -r)/
```

## Flag reference

| Flag | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `-a` | all information |
| `-s` | kernel name |
| `-n` | network hostname |
| `-r` | kernel release |
| `-v` | kernel version (build info) |
| `-m` | machine hardware architecture |
| `-p` | processor type |
| `-o` | operating system |
